+  "title": "Romper a: Breaking Into Sound and Tears",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "La perífrasis inceptiva *romper a + infinitivo* denota el comienzo repentino, ruidoso, violento o desbordante de una acción que quiebra un estado previo de quietud o contención. Se utiliza con un repertorio selecto de verbos que denotan expresiones vocales intensas (*romper a cantar*, *romper a gritar*, *romper a aplaudir*), fenómenos naturales tempestuosos (*romper a llover*, *romper a granizar*) o procesos de ebullición (*romper a hervir*)."
+      "content": "You know *empezar a*, *ponerse a* and *echarse a* + infinitive. *Romper a* + infinitive is a fourth way to say that something suddenly starts, and it has a special picture: the start breaks a silence or a calm. It is used with sounds and feelings that burst out, such as *llorar* (to cry), *reír* (to laugh), *gritar* (to shout), *aplaudir* (to applaud) and *llover* (to rain).\n\nUnlike *echarse*, *romper* is not reflexive, so there is no *se*. You meet it mostly in stories and news reports, usually in the preterite."
